bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510365 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1071 "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510409 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
